Scale changes the economics. ASIC design requires large upfront engineering and creates schedule risk. High volume spreads that cost across many chips; low volume or rapidly changing models favor reusable hardware.

A custom accelerator still needs foundry capacity, advanced packaging, HBM, networking, power and cooling. Owning the logic does not remove those bottlenecks.

5. How to make the comparison

Start with representative workloads, required output quality and a deployment horizon. Measure end-to-end latency, throughput, energy and total cost per acceptable output—not peak operations per second.

Then test change: a new model architecture, longer context, smaller batch or unsupported operator. A specialized design is attractive when its efficiency advantage survives realistic variation and covers enough volume to repay the development cost.

For an investor or operator, the evidence is workload adoption plus delivered economics. A benchmark chosen for the chip is weaker evidence than sustained utilization and customer savings on production models.

Quick check — answer before reading on

A service spends $12 million per year running a workload on GPUs. A custom ASIC would cost $30 million to design and would reduce annual running cost by 40%. Ignoring financing and risk, how long is the simple payback?

Answer: annual savings are $12 million × 40% = $4.8 million. Payback is $30 million ÷ $4.8 million = 6.25 years.

If the workload may change materially in two years, that payback is unattractive even if the ASIC is more efficient today. Volume, stability and time all matter.

Remember these three things

- GPUs trade some hardware efficiency for programmability across changing workloads.

- Custom ASICs can devote more silicon and power to expected work, but their advantage depends on utilization and software support.

- Compare acceptable outputs per dollar and watt over the deployment life, including development cost and the risk that workloads change.

